/cra.mr/mcp-skills-and-agents/\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's a comprehensive overview of Claude Code agent skills and MCP capabilities, and what sets it apart:\n\n## Claude Code Agent Skills\n\nSkills are folders containing instructions, scripts, and resources that Claude discovers and loads dynamically when relevant to a task—specialized training manuals that give Claude expertise in specific domains. Skills are portable, reusable capabilities that extend what Claude can do, working across Claude Code, Claude apps (web and mobile), and the API.\n\nKey features of Skills:\n- Skills use progressive disclosure to keep Claude efficient—when working on tasks, Claude first scans Skill metadata to identify relevant matches, then loads the full instructions if a Skill matches.\n- They're cheap, costing about 100 tokens of context until Claude decides it's needed, meaning you can have fifty skills installed and pay almost nothing for the ones you aren't using.\n\n## MCP (Model Context Protocol) Capabilities\n\nMCP is the open protocol for connecting AI to external systems—a universal adapter between LLMs and the tools they need. MCP connects Claude to external tools and data sources like GitHub, databases, and APIs, while Skills are for procedural knowledge and MCP is for external connectivity.\n\n## Key Differences from Chatbots\n\nClaude Code is a framework for orchestrating AI agents, and most people use one or two features without seeing how they stack together. Claude Code is not purely a coding tool: it's a tool for general computer automation—anything you can achieve by typing commands into a computer can now be automated by Claude Code.\n\nThe critical distinction from traditional chatbots:\n- MCPs give Claude capabilities, while Skills teach Claude workflows.\n- Skills teach Claude a procedure—a checklist, a house style, a repeatable workflow—while MCP is a live server connection that exposes real tools and data.\n\nSkills, MCP, and the Agent SDK aren'